    <h2>Execution Model</h2>
    <p>Each invocation has an independent execution frame containing its parameters, receiver when applicable, local variables, registers, and temporary values. Recursion and nested invocation create new frames. A return or propagated runtime error removes the frame's temporary state without changing the caller's live values.</p>
    <p>The abstract machine supports string values, boolean values, signed integer values, unsigned natural values, real values, void, and procedure values. Integer and natural values may use 8, 16, 32, or 64 bit widths. Real values may use 32-bit or 64-bit IEEE 754 formats.</p>
    <p>An interpreter may provide a stack. The stack is initially empty and supports querying its size, pushing a value onto the top of the stack, and popping the top value. Popping an empty stack is undefined.</p>
    <p>An interpreter may provide a status variable with the states success and failure. The initial state is success.</p>

    <h3>Calling Convention</h3>
    <p>The C backend emits typed procedure and method entry points. The first parameter is <code>Arcadia_Thread* thread</code>. A method additionally receives a pointer to its declaring class as <code>self</code>, followed by its declared parameters in declaration order. A procedure has no receiver parameter. There is no public argument-count/argument-array ABI. The C return type is the representation of the declared PDL result type.</p>
    <p><code>Arcadia.Void</code> results map to C <code>void</code>, scalar types map to their corresponding <code>Arcadia_*Value</code> types, class types map to pointers to their generated C structs, interface types map to <code>Arcadia_Object*</code>, enumeration types map to their generated C enumeration types, and <code>Arcadia.Value</code> maps directly to <code>Arcadia_Value</code>. A void parameter maps to <code>Arcadia_VoidValue</code>.</p>
    <p><code>Arcadia.Size</code> maps to <code>Arcadia_SizeValue</code> (<code>size_t</code>) in fields, parameters, dispatch slots, factories, native calls, and results. Its boxed representation is the runtime Size value tag, not a fixed-width Natural tag. Conversions validate that tag and preserve the full platform-sized range.</p>
    <p>Class and interface references are nullable at value boundaries: parameters (including constructor and native-target parameters), fields, and results. C <code>NULL</code> is boxed as <code>Arcadia_Value_makeVoidValue(Arcadia_VoidValue_Void)</code>, never as an object-reference value containing a null pointer; PDL <code>void</code> converts back to a typed null pointer. This uses the existing void literal and requires no additional nullable-type syntax. A non-null reference remains type-checked. Scalars and enumerations do not accept void in place of their declared value types.</p>
    <p>Receivers are not nullable: method dispatch and field access require an actual object. A void receiver is rejected rather than dispatched or dereferenced. Ordinary constructor allocation still uses its separate void-receiver convention. Nullable references in frames are void values, and object/interface visit callbacks skip null C fields; both representations are safe for GC.</p>
    <p>Virtual class dispatch slots use the same typed signature as their declaring methods. An override preserves the inherited slot signature. When its implementation receives a pointer to a derived class, the backend emits a forwarding thunk with the exact inherited receiver type instead of assigning an incompatible function pointer. A public virtual-method wrapper dispatches through the slot and therefore also invokes overrides. A nonvirtual method has no slot and its wrapper calls its implementation directly.</p>
    <p>An abstract method retains its typed public declaration and virtual slot, but has no generated private implementation body. Dispatch initialization sets an unimplemented abstract slot to null; a re-abstracting override explicitly clears an inherited concrete slot. Concrete overrides install their implementation or correctly typed forwarding thunk. Generated public virtual wrappers reject a null slot with <code>Arcadia_Status_OperationInvalid</code> and restore their value-stack frame on failure instead of invoking a null function pointer.</p>
    <p>Generated class method wrappers also validate non-null managed class and interface arguments before entering a bound override, including a raw native override without a generated implementation body. A reference of the wrong runtime type raises <code>Arcadia_Status_ArgumentTypeInvalid</code> without invoking the callback. Nullable arguments remain accepted; external reference and output pointers are forwarded as C pointers rather than treated as managed objects. Receiver and argument roots are established before type metadata lookup, and failures restore the caller's stack depth.</p>
    <p>Generated frames store live values in mutable slots of the runtime value stack, so reassignment in a loop does not accumulate roots. The receiver, parameters, local variables, registers, and temporary argument values remain GC roots while live. Generated visit callbacks trace object-valued fields. Jump-target cleanup restores the stack to its size at entry before propagating a failure.</p>
    <p>Native targets use the same typed parameter and result mapping as generated procedures and methods. The first parameter is <code>Arcadia_Thread*</code>, followed by the typed receiver pointer for a method or constructor, then the declared parameters in order. A native constructor returns C <code>void</code> and initializes the supplied object. Native implementations must follow the runtime rooting and error-propagation conventions.</p>
    <p>A method of a native class may bind to its existing conventional C entry-point name because that native class has no generated public wrapper. Calling such a method invokes the C target, which retains responsibility for any virtual dispatch or direct nonvirtual behavior. A native target must not collide with an actually generated public entry point or private method adapter.</p>
    <p>When generated code in module B needs a declaration defined in module A, it includes A's master header, such as <code>Example/A/Include.h</code>, rather than the individual type header. Selection follows the symbol's defining module, not a prefix guessed from its type name, and must not bypass A's private-header guard. This applies to foreign superclass definitions, enum signatures, source-side conversions and calls, and interface inheritance. Dependencies resolving to the same master header are deduplicated; forward declarations may avoid unnecessary complete-type includes.</p>
    <p>Within a module, generated dependencies on a native class use its explicit header path when supplied, including native calls and complete-type dependencies such as an embedded superclass. For a plain native class, the C backend does not generate or overwrite its externally owned header or implementation. Without an explicit mapping, the default qualified-name-derived header path remains in effect. Runtime built-ins are supplied through the runtime aggregate.</p>
    <p>A generated class may preserve an established C output path with <code>class "Path/To/Header.h" Qualified.Type</code>, optionally preceded by <code>abstract</code>. The mapping is interoperability metadata: it changes neither the program's type name nor its fields or inheritance. It must be a safe relative forward-slash path ending in <code>.h</code>. The backend owns that header and the corresponding <code>.c.g</code> source beside it; include guards follow the mapped header path. Duplicate output ownership, replacing a module aggregate, and replacing an opaque native declaration remain errors.</p>
    <p>For <code>native implementation</code> classes, normal C backend generation emits the actual <code>.h</code> declaration file under the owning module's <code>Library/Sources</code> tree. It emits no <code>.c</code> or <code>.c.g</code> implementation and no synthetic factory. Header guards follow the mapped file path, including legacy path components absent from the runtime type name. Native enums sharing that header are emitted once before class declarations; opaque native classes retain externally owned headers. This behavior is part of ordinary compiler generation, not a test-only preview or header-copying step.</p>
    <p>A generated enumeration may likewise preserve its established C path with <code>enumeration "Path/To/Header.h" Qualified.Type</code>. The path obeys the same safe-relative-path and <code>.h</code> restrictions as a generated class mapping. The backend owns that header and its adjacent ordinary <code>.c</code> source (not <code>.c.g</code>); guards and source includes follow the mapped path while runtime and C type names remain unchanged. Mappings participate in output ownership validation: they cannot replace module aggregates, share another generated header, or overwrite opaque native declarations. Dependencies from another module still include that module's master header, not the mapped per-enum header.</p>
    <p>Native enumeration declarations preserve their existing C type names and use their mapped native headers, including when a generated class stores such an enum in a field. They have no generated header/source output path. Executable value transport requires the native provider to export its Arcadia runtime enumeration descriptor through the conventional <code>_&lt;CType&gt;_getType(thread)</code> function, for example using <code>Arcadia_declareEnumerationType</code> and <code>Arcadia_defineEnumerationType</code>. The boxed representation is the existing <code>Arcadia.EnumerationValue</code>, carrying that exact descriptor and an Integer32 numeric payload. An integer or another enum type with the same numeric value is not interchangeable with the declared enum.</p>
    <p>The native enum value ABI must be no wider than <code>Arcadia_Integer32Value</code>, enforced by a generated C static assertion whenever executable transport uses the type. Boxing checks the numeric value before narrowing; unboxing checks exact enum identity and that conversion to the native C type preserves the numeric value. Values outside the shared representable range raise <code>ArgumentTypeInvalid</code>. This also supports native scalar-alias/macro enum APIs that export an Arcadia enumeration descriptor. Native declarations alone neither manufacture descriptors nor regenerate the native enum's headers, values, aliases or implementation.</p>
    <p>For native declaration compatibility, the C backend maps external scalar references to pointers to the scalar representation and untyped storage references to <code>void*</code>. Read-only views map to const-qualified referents. Output references remain writable pointers. A value record field becomes an embedded anonymous C struct. Inheritance supplies the C base members implicitly; virtual methods supply dispatch slots in declaration order, while nonvirtual methods have direct entry points and no slot. Class constants become prefixed macros. These are C-backend choices, not declarations of C layout in PDL.</p>
    <p>The current C backend supports these record/ownership descriptions for native class fields and the qualified parameter declarations of native bindings. It does not yet transport external references or record values through its <code>Arcadia_Value</code> execution slots; such executable uses are diagnosed. Read-only object inputs to native bindings retain normal nullable-object boxing and GC rooting. Native fields with borrowed, owned, or retained lifetime contracts continue to use their existing native implementation, rather than silently acquiring generated ownership behavior.</p>
    <p>Generated abstract-method C wrappers may forward qualified typed parameters directly to native overrides: external references remain C pointers and never enter value slots, while readonly object arguments retain their GC roots and const-qualified dispatch types. This permits compatible wrappers for byte inputs and scalar output parameters. Invoking an external-reference operation from an executable PDL body still requires a supported value representation and is diagnosed otherwise.</p>
    <p><em>Planned native interoperability approach:</em> external byte storage will be exposed through native class objects, for example <code>Arcadia_ByteArray</code> or <code>Arcadia_ImmutableByteArray</code>, rather than adding raw <code>void*</code> storage to <code>Arcadia.Value</code>. This is an implementation plan, not a currently available external-reference execution feature; it is deferred until generated implementation bodies need that transport.</p>
    <p>Constructors are private initialization operations, not value-returning public functions. The runtime constructor callback returns <code>void</code> and takes <code>thread</code> and a typed <code>self</code>. It follows the runtime value-stack convention: callers push arguments in declaration order, then their <code>Arcadia.Natural8</code> count; a successful callback consumes the count and arguments. A generated concrete non-native class exposes a typed <code>Class_create(thread, parameters...)</code> factory returning <code>Class*</code>. The factory adapts its typed parameters to the runtime constructor protocol. Abstract classes and native/native-implementation declarations have no synthesized factory.</p>
    <p>At constructor entry, the top-relative offset <code>0</code> holds the count, the last argument is at <code>1</code>, and the first of <code>n</code> arguments is at <code>n</code>. Thus a three-argument <code>(timestamp, horizontal, vertical)</code> callback reads offsets <code>3</code>, <code>2</code>, and <code>1</code>. Forwarding a superclass call pushes a separate copied argument segment and count; successful superclass construction consumes that segment while the derived callback's original arguments remain available. Stack-depth snapshots and offsets are size values, distinct from the Natural8 argument-count value.</p>
    <p>For enumeration parameters, including supported native enumerations, the runtime constructor callback accepts either an enumeration value carrying the exact declared enum type or a legacy C <code>Arcadia.Integer32</code> value. The callback normalizes the latter to the declared enum type before invoking the typed initialization operation. This compatibility adaptation applies only to the runtime callback boundary: PDL <code>construct</code> arguments, including explicit superclass forwarding, still require the exact enumeration type and reject raw integers and values of other enum types.</p>
    <p>A fully qualified enumeration constant used as an operand produces a boxed value carrying its declaring enumeration descriptor and Integer32 payload, not an untyped integer literal. This applies to generated and supported native enum declarations; native constants retain the existing native ABI/range checks. Constants may be assigned to registers or passed, compared, constructed with, and returned, subject to ordinary exact-type checks. Existing local/parameter lookup retains precedence, and a constant cannot be an instruction target. Foreign constants require their defining module's master header.</p>
    <p>Equality instructions retain registered scalar comparison callbacks when present. If an enumeration on the left has no such callback, <code>isEqualTo</code> compares exact enumeration identity and payload; a non-enum right operand is unequal. <code>isNotEqualTo</code> is the inverse for that fallback. This does not add enumeration arithmetic or ordering, nor does it change object comparison dispatch.</p>
    <p>Generated functions root incoming object/interface receivers and parameters and general-value parameters before any operation that can collect, including enum/type metadata resolution. Internal PDL registers and mutable parameters may remain value-stack slots. Calls convert those slots to typed arguments and box typed results as needed; this internal representation does not change the public C interface. Errors continue to propagate through the runtime status/jump mechanism, including from functions returning <code>void</code>.</p>
    <p>The backend writes class and procedure headers directly as <code>.h</code>, just like interface and enumeration headers. Header dependencies refer directly to those files; no header-copying or installation step is required. Class and procedure sources retain the experimental <code>.c.g</code> suffix. Plain opaque native classes retain externally owned definitions; native-implementation classes regenerate their declaration headers but retain native executable bodies. Header declarations include or forward-declare their typed parameter/result dependencies.</p>
    <p>Procedure output paths follow the declared identifier. For example, procedure <code>M.N.p</code> produces <code>Library/Sources/M/N/p.h</code> and <code>Library/Sources/M/N/p.c.g</code> within its module, with C entry point <code>M_N_p</code>. The enclosing module is not prefixed a second time. Existing short-name procedures retain their flat output paths and module-prefixed C names; qualified identifiers should be used for namespaced output. Native target names remain verbatim. Generated includes use the same output metadata, and foreign dependencies still use the owning module's master include. A procedure output that would replace a module aggregate or another declaration's header is rejected before files are written.</p>
    <p>Abstract classes retain their object and dispatch layouts, type registration, visit callbacks, and constructor implementations, but have no generated <code>_create</code> declaration or definition. Superclass initialization calls the parent's constructor implementation on the existing receiver; it does not use an allocation entry point. This also applies to abstract parents.</p>
    <p>A void parameter of a native target uses <code>Arcadia_VoidValue</code>; only a void result uses C <code>void</code>.</p>

    <h3>Generated Member Names</h3>
    <p>Generated fields and dispatch slots preserve their declared PDL names, except that one <code>_pdl</code> suffix is appended when the original name is a C/C++ keyword, is <code>_parent</code>, or already ends in <code>_pdl</code>. This rule is applied exactly once to the original name, not repeatedly to the result. The backend uses a fixed keyword set covering C23 and C++23. Names containing <code>__</code> or beginning with an underscore followed by an uppercase ASCII letter are reserved by C/C++ and are diagnosed by the C backend.</p>
    <p>For example, <code>timestamp</code> remains <code>timestamp</code>, <code>switch</code> becomes <code>switch_pdl</code>, <code>switch_pdl</code> becomes <code>switch_pdl_pdl</code>, <code>_parent</code> becomes <code>_parent_pdl</code>, and <code>_parent_pdl</code> becomes <code>_parent_pdl_pdl</code>. The generated base member itself is named <code>_parent</code>. Declarations, accesses, initialization, GC visits, and dispatch operations use the same mapping. Native code accessing generated members must use their mapped C names.</p>
    <p>Parameter names in generated headers use the same escaping rule and additionally escape <code>thread</code> and <code>self</code> to avoid the generated calling-convention parameter names. Source implementations may use positional parameter names without changing the C function type.</p>

    <h3>Arithmetic</h3>
    <p>For unsigned natural values of width <var>n</var> and <var>m</var>, <code>add</code>, <code>subtract</code>, and <code>multiply</code> compute the mathematical result modulo 2<sup>k</sup>, where <var>k = max(n,m)</var>. The result is represented as an unsigned natural value of width <var>k</var>.</p>
    <p>For signed integer values of width <var>n</var> and <var>m</var>, operands are sign-extended to width <var>k = max(n,m)</var>. Addition, subtraction, and multiplication use two's-complement arithmetic and keep the low-order <var>k</var> bits of the mathematical result.</p>
    <p>For division, division by zero raises an arithmetic error. Otherwise the operation computes the quotient according to the selected integer or natural arithmetic model and represents it at width <var>k = max(n,m)</var>.</p>
    <p>The unary <code>negate</code> operation computes zero minus its operand in the operand's arithmetic domain. The unary <code>not</code> operation is the logical negation operation.</p>
